or a farmer this is super  critical because  this is the period now where it's almost  you know make or break live or die  october is soon and if we don't have a  rain again on november  like the next year will be  even um even worse so how did madagascar  end up here well there are lots of  reasons for decades the governments have  been pretty unstable and the country  ranks near the bottom in a lot of  important categories whether it's income  corruption or what the world bank calls  human capital basically whether a  country invests in things like education  and health that's the baseline  then you add covid19 the complications  that brings  and then there's climate change  [Music]  so we know that greenhouse gas emissions  are leading to more extreme weather that  was one of the big conclusions in a  recent report by the ipcc the world's  top climate scientists and when it comes  to madagascar there are really two  things going on one on land one at sea  let's start on land southern madagascar  is already pretty dry but the last six  years have been exceptionally warm when  the air is warmer the molecules move  further apart and the air can hold more  water vapor which means it can suck up  more moisture from the land  so if the land is already dry because of  a lack of rain  warmer air makes it all worse it's like  the atmosphere is thirstier  now what's happening in the sea is that  global warming is creating pockets of  water that are exceptionally warm  that changes the wind patterns and leads  to more rain in those areas  climate change made the eastern pacific  you know much much warmer in 2015-16  which produced a massive drought all  across southern africa and in southern  madagascar and that was the kind of  beginning of this drought  everyone knows that there's a change  like um  it's getting dry and drier it basically  is people living on the front line of  climate change  because they've had no rain no decent  water  that they are now  absolutely living  living in the most you know the most  minimum conditions they have nothing  this is what environmentalists mean when  they talk about climate justice  madagascar produces just 0.01  of all global carbon emissions but look  what's happening there are people who  have done nothing to contribute to  climate change and yet they're burying  the brunt of of something that's that  that's actually you know having a you  know colossal impact on on their lives  one of the things that wealthier nations  can do  is to work  uh you know with these insecure nations  proactively  to  develop ways to manage weather and  climate risk that issue is going to be  on the table at cop26 the major u.n  climate summit that's happening in  november we need to go to the roots of  the economic crisis and we need to treat  it as a crisis and unless the people in  power are willing to do that now in cop  then it will just continue like now uh  it's no longer a matter of  you know  debating whether uh action had to be  taken now in five years time or ten  years time it's a matter of taking  action at the moment right now i think  we all share this experience of like  holy moly this has gotten a lot worse  really fast unless you know we mitigate  and we do submissions  really quickly we're going to see more  of the same in the very near future  right now in madagascar humanitarian  groups are working hard to get food to  the people who need it most but the  bigger questions aren't going away  like how we make sure millions more  don't starve because of what we're doing  to the planet  if you're interested in the debate about  how to protect the environment check out  our recent start here episode on ecocide  and be sure to subscribe to al jazeera  so you don't miss our new episodes and  if there's a topic you think we should  be covering let us know in the comments  [Music]
 

